\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Square-integrable cell density}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Fix an integer #{p\ge 2} and real numbers #{\lambda>0} and
#{m>0} with #{0<p\lambda<1}. Let #{X} and #{\eta} be the
inverse limit and cylinder probability of \ref{fdip-00FE},
and let #{(x_k)} be a coherent sequence with the projective
law of \ref{fdip-00C3}. Regard the cell density #{F_k} of
\ref{fdip-00FA} as the function on #{X} that takes the value
#{F_k(r)} on the depth-#{k} cylinder of #{r}. Then
#{(F_k)_{k\ge 0}} converges in
#{L^2(\Omega;L^2(X,\eta))} to a centered Gaussian random
element #{F} of #{L^2(X,\eta)}. For every residue #{r} modulo
#{p^k},
##{\int_{C(r)}F\,d\eta=M_k(r)}
in #{L^2(\Omega)}. The same identity extends by linearity to
every function constant on the cylinders of some finite
depth.}

\proof{
\p{Set #{F_0=x_0}. For this one-point residue ring the factor
#{(p\lambda)^{0}} equals #{1}, so #{F_0} is the depth-zero
case of the scaling in \ref{fdip-00FA}. The cylinder #{C(r)} of depth #{k} has measure
#{p^{-k}} and splits into #{p} cylinders of depth #{k+1}, of
equal measure.
On a coherent sequence, \ref{fdip-00FA} says that #{F_k(r)}
is the arithmetic mean of those #{p} finer values. The
conditional expectation of #{F_{k+1}} onto the depth-#{k}
cylinders is therefore #{F_k}. The difference
#{F_{k+1}-F_k} is orthogonal in #{L^2(X,\eta)} to every
function constant on depth-#{k} cylinders. At depth #{k+1},
\ref{fdip-00FC} assigns this difference the index
#{\ell=k}, hence the projector #{P_0}. That image is
orthogonal to every function constant on the classes modulo
#{p^k}.}

\p{The expected Haar square of the difference equals the scale
mass #{(p-1)\lambda(p\lambda)^k} of \ref{fdip-00FD}. These
differences are orthogonal in #{L^2(\Omega;L^2(X,\eta))}, so
the expected square of a sum of them is the sum of the
expected squares. The numerical series
#{\sum_k(p\lambda)^k} converges because #{0<p\lambda<1}. The
partial sums #{F_k} are therefore Cauchy in
#{L^2(\Omega;L^2(X,\eta))} and converge to some #{F}.}

\p{For #{f\in L^2(X,\eta)}, the scalar
#{\langle F_k,f\rangle_{\eta}} is centered Gaussian. Its
variance converges because #{F_k} converges in
#{L^2(\Omega)}. The characteristic function
#{\exp(-t^2 v_k/2)} converges pointwise to
#{\exp(-t^2 v/2)}. Convergence in #{L^2(\Omega)} implies
convergence in distribution, so
#{\langle F,f\rangle_{\eta}} is centered Gaussian of variance
#{v}.}

\p{The integral of #{F_k} over #{C(r)} equals
#{p^{-k}F_k(r)=M_k(r)}. For every #{m\ge k} the conditional
expectation identity gives the same integral for #{F_m}. The
linear functional #{g\mapsto\int_{C(r)}g\,d\eta} is
continuous on #{L^2(X,\eta)}, of operator norm #{p^{-k/2}}.
Passing to the #{L^2(\Omega)} limit yields the stated
equality. A function constant on depth-#{k} cylinders is a
finite linear combination of the indicators of those
cylinders.}
}
